use std
into species.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with sp_ is exported). It supplies the per-node terms of dilute species transport (diffusion with first-order decay, a uniform source and production from a parent species that decays into this one) on a grid whose node volumes JavaScript fills, and it sums the concentration field into the numbers a report needs. Concentrations are moles per cubic meter, volumes cubic millimeters, rates per second. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn sp_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "sp_view_f64";
```

Module state. Int `NN` (node count), starting at 0. Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (16 inputs), `OUT` (16 outputs), `V` (1 per node, node volume), `C` (1 per node, this species' concentration), `PC` (1 per node, the parent species' concentration), `DA` (1 per node, diagonal terms out) and `QF` (1 per node, source terms out).

Sizing helper. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again.

The exported function `sp_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes through sp_view_f64: 1 `IN`, 2 `OUT`, 3 `V`, 4 `C`, 5 `PC`, 7 `DA`, 8 `QF`. It returns 0.

The exported function `sp_init()`, declared with the export keyword, sizes `IN` and `OUT` to 16 zeros each with zf, then calls `sp_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `sp_size(nn)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 node count, sets `NN` to it, sizes `V`, `C`, `PC`, `DA` and `QF` to nn zeros each with zf, then calls `sp_views()` and returns 0.

The exported function `sp_terms()`, declared with the export keyword, reads k (decay rate) from IN[0], r (uniform source) from IN[1] and kp (the parent's decay rate times the yield into this species) from IN[2]. For each node n below `NN`, with v equal to V[n]: DA[n] becomes k times v, and QF[n] becomes (r plus kp times PC[n]) times v. It then sets OUT[0] to the sum over all nodes of QF[n] and returns 0.

The exported function `sp_stats()`, declared with the export keyword, sums the concentration field over nodes whose volume V[n] is above 0: lo starts at 1e300 and hi at -1e300, and amount, vol and decay start at 0.0. For each such node n with c equal to C[n]: lo becomes the smaller of lo and c, hi the larger of hi and c, amount grows by c times V[n], vol grows by V[n], and decay grows by IN[0] times c times V[n]. When vol is 0.0, lo and hi become 0.0. It sets OUT[1] to lo, OUT[2] to hi, OUT[3] to amount times 1e-9 (moles), OUT[4] to amount divided by vol when vol is above 0 and else 0.0 (the mean concentration), OUT[5] to vol and OUT[7] to decay times 1e-9 (moles per second consumed by decay), and returns 0.

`main()` does nothing.
